Abstract = {Does the situation in Afghanistan amount to persecution due to the
   accumulation of repressive measures enacted against women and is it
   sufficient that women are affected by such measures merely on the basis
   of their gender? While these two questions remain in front of the Court
   of Justice of the EU, several EU member states have moved to grant
   asylum to women and girls from Afghanistan on general risks of
   gender-based persecution. This dynamism in asylum regulations across the
   EU has occurred alongside renewed discussions around gender-based
   persecution and gender apartheid in international criminal law in light
   of the ongoing process for a Crimes Against Humanity Treaty. In this
   article, I put these developments into conversation through case study
   method and legal-institutional analysis, and argue that the historical
   link between international refugee law and international criminal law
   provides a space to envision an integrated system of protection around
   gender-related claims. To this end, I offer three outlooks for
   establishing general risks of gender-based persecution in international
   refugee law and the relationship between gender apartheid and asylum.},
